WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.001
In just a couple of seconds

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:04.629
Spain is about to get its first
total solar eclipse in over 100 years.

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:08.425
And we're here with NASA
to film it from space.

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:12.262
But we almost didn't do this video
because it turns out

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:15.598
there are a great couple videos
on solar eclipses on YouTube,

00:00:15.598 --> 00:00:17.225
and we just thought
we couldn't add anything.

00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:19.436
But then we started looking.

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:20.937
[laughs]

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:22.313
Oh my God, that's insane!

00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:23.940
And it turns out...

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.359
Oh, I can't, I just gotta go watch this.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.028
That's insane.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:31.364
So, Henry, Emilia and I still had

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:33.116
some unanswered questions like,

00:00:33.116 --> 00:00:34.743
why is the Northern Hemisphere

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:37.537
getting more total eclipses
than the southern hemisphere?

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:42.208
Why does the sun suddenly turn white
only during pure totality?

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:43.501
And how come

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:45.545
we still haven't figured
out what causes

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.881
these elusive shadow bands
on the ground?

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:50.216
Oh, that's so weird.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.094
Okay, so we are on the
way to Burgos, Spain,

00:00:53.094 --> 00:00:56.097
because it's going to be right
in the center of the moon's shadow.

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:57.265
So perfect for totality.

00:00:57.265 --> 00:00:59.893
And we're also meeting up with NASA
and a bunch of other science groups

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:02.353
because they're launching a couple
of balloons up to the edge of space.

00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:04.105
So they will actually be
filming the eclipse,

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:06.399
and hopefully they can
answer some of our questions.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:07.275
Vamos!

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.740
Oh my God!

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.117
It's getting occluded, oh no!

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:17.202
Angela?
- Yes.

00:01:17.202 --> 00:01:18.036
Oh, lovely to meet you.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:18.912
I’m Emilia.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:20.038
Oh, so nice to meet you.

00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:21.081
This is Gregor.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.832
Nice to meet you,
thanks for having us.

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:23.750
So, can I ask you?

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.000
Yeah.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.086
Are those total
solar eclipses on your toes?

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:28.129
Yes. Totally geeking it up.

00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:30.006
Yeah,
I guess, first question right off the bat.

00:01:30.006 --> 00:01:31.424
How many eclipses have you seen?

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.635
So this will be
my fourth eclipse

00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:35.261
and the third
total eclipse.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:38.264
There's lots of folks here
who have seen many more than me.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.809
Eight or even as many as 12. That's crazy.

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:43.019
So how rare is it
that Spain is getting two

00:01:43.019 --> 00:01:44.979
total solar eclipses in
less than 12 months?

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.108
If you picked any random point
on any place on the earth,

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:53.655
it's about every 300 years
that the eclipse will repeat there.

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:57.158
But then there are some places, you know,
where there happens to be occurrences,

00:01:57.158 --> 00:02:00.161
because there is kind of a pattern
to the eclipses.

00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:04.457
You can see one of those patterns if you map out the paths of all total solar eclipses

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:07.919
between 2000 BCE and 3000 CE.

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:11.631
Notice that the Northern Hemisphere
consistently gets more eclipses,

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:14.634
about 15% more than the
Southern Hemisphere.

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:17.428
Now for there to be a
total solar eclipse

00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:19.430
where the moon completely
covers the sun,

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.433
you want it to be
bigger than the sun in the sky.

00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:25.770
Now, the biggest factor for
that is the moon's elliptical orbit.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.398
Sometimes it's further from the Earth,
sometimes closer.

00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:32.986
So over the course of the month,
its apparent area increases by up to 30%.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:36.114
But the Earth's orbit around
the sun is also elliptical.

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:40.326
So from our point of view,
the sun's area is about 7% smaller in

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.245
July than it is in January.

00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:46.207
Which means there is a higher chance
of a total solar eclipse around July.

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:49.043
But that's also when it's mostly
the north half of Earth

00:02:49.043 --> 00:02:52.213
that is tilted towards the sun,
the Northern Hemisphere summer.

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:55.425
So that's where the total eclipses
tend to fall.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:57.886
Now the Southern Hemisphere
is actually going to get

00:02:57.886 --> 00:02:59.304
more annular eclipses

00:02:59.304 --> 00:03:01.639
where the moon is slightly
smaller than the sun

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:03.933
so you have this ring of sunlight
around it.

00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:08.188
And that's because during the southern
summer the sun is actually closer.

00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:11.858
All of this is actually going to flip,
because the Earth precesses around

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:16.279
its axis and its elliptical orbit
also slowly shifts around the sun.

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:18.364
In about 9500 years,

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:21.868
it will be the Southern Hemisphere
that gets more total solar eclipses.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:26.456
Now, there is another interesting pattern
that shows up when you look at all

00:03:26.456 --> 00:03:29.792
types of solar eclipses,
including annular and partial ones too.

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:33.254
So I crunched some numbers,
like 5000 years of eclipses.

00:03:33.254 --> 00:03:38.051
And if you look at how many happened
per year, there's never a year

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:41.304
without some sort of solar eclipse,
which kind of feels mind boggling.

00:03:41.304 --> 00:03:42.555
You at least have to get two

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.933
and sometimes you can get five.

00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:47.310
How do you get five?
- Yeah, yeah I know.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.436
Check this out, so:

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:52.023
The moon's orbit is tilted about
five degrees out of the Earth sun plane.

00:03:52.023 --> 00:03:54.525
So most times the moon passes
in front of the sun

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:56.945
It's at the wrong height
to cast a shadow on Earth.

00:03:56.945 --> 00:03:59.489
And that's why we don't get
a solar eclipse every month.

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:03.618
But there are these two nodes
along the moon's orbit where it does

00:04:03.618 --> 00:04:05.370
actually cross the Earth-Sun plane.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.454
And throughout the year,

00:04:06.454 --> 00:04:10.166
both of these nodes are going to land
nicely in between the Earth and the Sun.

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:13.378
This happens about six months apart,
and it opens a window

00:04:13.378 --> 00:04:16.798
of around 34 days
where solar eclipses become possible.

00:04:16.798 --> 00:04:19.550
And these are called eclipse seasons.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.636
This is huge window to get eclipses.
- Yeah.

00:04:21.636 --> 00:04:26.349
So as long as you get a new moon
somewhere inside of this eclipse window,

00:04:26.349 --> 00:04:27.225
you get an eclipse.

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:29.686
But it turns out
you have to get at least one.

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:30.228
The reason is

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:34.857
the eclipse season is 34 days,
but a new moon happens every 29.5 days.

00:04:34.857 --> 00:04:35.650
Of course.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.279
So you can't go through an eclipse season
without the new moon happening

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:40.238
at least once.

00:04:40.238 --> 00:04:41.572
However you roll the dice,

00:04:41.572 --> 00:04:45.201
it always has to land
inside the eclipse season at least once.

00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:47.745
So you always get two a year.
Great.

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:48.663
Yeah, right?

00:04:48.663 --> 00:04:52.875
Now, if the new moon falls
towards the middle of an eclipse season,

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:56.045
it usually produces
a total or an annular eclipse,

00:04:56.045 --> 00:05:00.300
and you can only fit one of those types
of eclipses into a season.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.719
So you can get about two per year.

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:04.304
Yeah, this doesn't seem rare,

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:05.763
definitely not once in a lifetime.

00:05:05.763 --> 00:05:08.224
Yeah, but it gets worse
because if you also count

00:05:08.224 --> 00:05:10.727
partial eclipses,
those happen more towards the edges.

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:12.145
And because they happen to the edges.

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.605
You can get two
in a single eclipse season.

00:05:14.605 --> 00:05:16.649
Or you can get four a year.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.943
But wait, then how do you get five?

00:05:18.943 --> 00:05:19.944
Yeah. The thing is,

00:05:19.944 --> 00:05:23.740
the eclipse seasons themselves
also drift around the Earth's orbit.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:24.991
So if you start on January 1st

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:28.077
and you get an eclipse
really, really soon into the year,

00:05:28.077 --> 00:05:30.163
you can hit like a partial
and then another partial.

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:32.874
And then as you're moving,
these eclipse seasons are shifting.

00:05:32.874 --> 00:05:36.085
So you get another one and another one
and then you think you're done.

00:05:36.085 --> 00:05:38.588
But the eclipse season has actually
already shifted into December.

00:05:38.588 --> 00:05:40.840
And then you get a final one.
So you can get up to five.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:41.924
Woah.

00:05:44.135 --> 00:05:46.971
Okay, wait, I realized
I forgot to tell you

00:05:46.971 --> 00:05:49.974
my favorite fact out of this, out
of this whole discussion, and that's that

00:05:49.974 --> 00:05:54.479
if you look at the eclipse season,
when the eclipse happens,

00:05:54.479 --> 00:05:59.692
the solar eclipse here,
and you rewind by about two weeks,

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:03.654
you'll realize that now the Earth is in
between the sun and the moon.

00:06:03.654 --> 00:06:05.156
So you get a lunar eclipse.

00:06:05.156 --> 00:06:08.743
So you get a lunar eclipse, which means

00:06:08.743 --> 00:06:11.037
every time there is a solar eclipse,

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:14.916
it is accompanied by a lunar eclipse
either two weeks before or after.

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:15.833
Oh, that's so cool. Yeah.

00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:18.211
So there's one happening in two weeks.

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:21.005
Oh, amazing. Yeah, yeah, it's really cool.

00:06:21.005 --> 00:06:23.883
I actually tried
to find an answer to this question,

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:27.095
but if you look at timelapses
taken during the eclipse.

00:06:27.095 --> 00:06:29.931
You'll see that the partial parts
are usually like yellowish in color.

00:06:29.931 --> 00:06:31.766
And then the totality is white.

00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:35.603
If you Google on Google you get like a little animation and it turns white.

00:06:35.603 --> 00:06:38.981
Yellow, and then it's white
and then it's yellow again.

00:06:38.981 --> 00:06:42.735
I could not find a satisfying scientific
explanation for why that happens.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:43.986
It's so bright, right?

00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:45.988
In order to take those timelapse pictures,

00:06:45.988 --> 00:06:47.782
you have to put a filter in front of it.

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:53.204
And so the most common with those filters,
it's that kind of reddish orange color.

00:06:53.204 --> 00:06:54.747
So it's just the most common

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:58.543
filter makes it that color
because the sun is white.

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:02.004
But then it's dim enough that we can look
right at it during totality.

00:07:02.004 --> 00:07:04.006
And so you see white.
- Yeah

00:07:04.006 --> 00:07:05.299
It's just a filter, okay.

00:07:05.299 --> 00:07:06.634
It's just the filter.

00:07:06.634 --> 00:07:09.178
Maybe I can show you where we're at. Sure.

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:11.222
You can kind of get set up. Hey.

00:07:13.558 --> 00:07:15.935
We're a NASA funded lab in Bozeman, Montana,

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:18.688
and this is an extension of what's called
the

00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:20.106
Nationwide Eclipse Ballooning Project.

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:22.984
So we studied the 2023 and 2024 eclipse.

00:07:22.984 --> 00:07:26.112
So we have two teams
in Reykjavik, Iceland, and we have three

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:27.989
teams here in Spain.

00:07:27.989 --> 00:07:31.409
The Iceland teams are launching
essentially weather balloons, radiosondes.

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:34.829
And then we're launching what we call
larger engineering balloons here.

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:36.789
Look, cowboy engineering here.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:37.999
What are you doing?

00:07:37.999 --> 00:07:42.170
We attach this to our basically
the balloon.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:45.548
Like right below where we're connecting
the balloon to the payload.

00:07:45.548 --> 00:07:48.509
And usually we have a little bit
more sophisticated weights,

00:07:48.509 --> 00:07:51.429
but we didn't want to just ship
weights to Spain with us.

00:07:51.429 --> 00:07:54.432
So we took water bottles
and filled them with rocks.

00:07:54.432 --> 00:07:56.142
I love it, I love it.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:58.478
We have a little bag of like, souvenir

00:07:58.478 --> 00:08:01.564
kind of things, like signed NASA stickers
and things like that

00:08:01.564 --> 00:08:03.232
that we're going
to send up on the balloon.

00:08:03.232 --> 00:08:05.818
This is going up so you can sign it.
- Hey!

00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:10.573
You're signing it ‘Eclipse’?

00:08:10.573 --> 00:08:12.450
I mean, Eclipse 2026,
I want to show it off

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.535
I thought you were going to say
like ‘Henry’ or something.

00:08:14.535 --> 00:08:17.413
So, “Henry was here”?

00:08:17.413 --> 00:08:20.416
We're not putting you
in the balloon, though, so...

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:22.043
We're gonna set up kind of in the middle

00:08:22.043 --> 00:08:23.628
just because of the wind direction
at the moment.

00:08:23.628 --> 00:08:24.587
Okay.

00:08:24.587 --> 00:08:28.716
So right now the wind's going
the wrong way from the forecast.

00:08:28.716 --> 00:08:30.843
Right. Blowing it that way.

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:33.513
It’s just finding a way to be far enough
that we don't hit the church.

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:36.182
How strong does the wind
have to be for you to hit the church?

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.267
Like it's pretty far away, no?

00:08:38.267 --> 00:08:40.436
It is. We just have to outclimb it
before we get there.

00:08:40.436 --> 00:08:42.021
Okay.

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:50.446
It's kind of weird.

00:08:50.446 --> 00:08:52.031
I feel like
it was one of those things

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:55.535
where I'd seen pictures of them,
but I didn't realize how much it

00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:58.704
just looked like a regular balloon,
but just super sized.

00:09:03.626 --> 00:09:05.628
On the count of three,

00:09:05.628 --> 00:09:08.631
one, two, three.

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:16.138
Amazing.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.311
That launch went absolutely perfectly,

00:09:22.311 --> 00:09:25.022
and right now our cameras are on their way

00:09:25.022 --> 00:09:27.608
up to the edge of space.

00:09:27.608 --> 00:09:29.610
But about 3000km away

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.613
NASA's launching
another eclipse mission, one mounted

00:09:32.613 --> 00:09:36.075
on a 70 year old Cold War
era bomber plane.

00:09:36.075 --> 00:09:38.995
They're trying to chase the totality
for as long as they can,

00:09:38.995 --> 00:09:42.290
but surprisingly,
this is actually already been attempted.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.376
In 1973, the Concorde went Mach 2

00:09:45.376 --> 00:09:48.462
and stayed in the totality for 74 minutes.

00:09:48.462 --> 00:09:50.506
Here on the ground, the longest
anyone's going to be able to see

00:09:50.506 --> 00:09:52.675
the totality is two minutes
and 18 seconds.

00:09:52.675 --> 00:09:56.762
And with NASA's jet going at 460mph,
they're going to be able

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:58.931
to stay in the totality for

00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:00.600
just around three minutes.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:04.228
It doesn't seem worth it,
but they're not just trying to stay in it longer.

00:10:04.228 --> 00:10:06.355
Compared to a balloon,
which is a bit shaky.

00:10:06.355 --> 00:10:09.650
A jet is much more stable, so it lets them
mount much more sensitive equipment

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.903
so they can study
a very specific part of the sun,

00:10:12.903 --> 00:10:16.324
part of the sun, that once led to the
discovery of a fake element.

00:10:17.283 --> 00:10:20.536
On the total eclipse of
the 18th of August, 1868,

00:10:20.536 --> 00:10:22.872
French astronomer
Jules Janssen went to India

00:10:22.872 --> 00:10:26.626
to look at a part of the sun
that's normally outshone from observation,

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:30.588
the prominences.
Arcs of glowing gas at the very edge.

00:10:30.588 --> 00:10:34.634
He put a slit in front of a prism
and broke their light into its constituent wavelengths.

00:10:34.634 --> 00:10:36.636
Out came five bright bands.

00:10:36.636 --> 00:10:39.722
One of them matched no element
known on Earth.

00:10:39.722 --> 00:10:43.392
Two months later, the English astronomer
Norman Lockyer saw the same line.

00:10:43.392 --> 00:10:47.438
He called it helium, after Helios,
the Greek god of the sun.

00:10:47.438 --> 00:10:48.773
The next year, other scientists

00:10:48.773 --> 00:10:53.027
used the same method on the hotter corona
and found another unexplained band.

00:10:53.027 --> 00:10:56.280
For 70 years it was thought to be
an element called coronium,

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:00.910
but in 1939 it turned out to be iron,
so hot that 13 of its electrons

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.078
had been ripped away.

00:11:02.078 --> 00:11:06.874
So an eclipse led to discovering
both a real element and a fake one.

00:11:06.874 --> 00:11:10.711
Now, to study the eclipse, Janssen used a slit,
but you actually don't have to.

00:11:12.088 --> 00:11:14.590
So here I've cut out a triangle shape
in this cardboard,

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:17.134
and we're going to see the shadow
that it casts.

00:11:17.134 --> 00:11:17.927
You can see there's

00:11:17.927 --> 00:11:21.806
this projection of light
that makes a triangle, not too surprising,

00:11:21.806 --> 00:11:25.935
but watch what happens as I slowly
move it away from the ground.

00:11:25.935 --> 00:11:30.022
You'll see that triangle slowly morphs
into a circle.

00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:32.650
I can even take this eclipse shape,
which looks like an eclipse

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:36.904
when it's close to the ground, but
I pull it again and it's always a circle.

00:11:37.822 --> 00:11:39.615
Gregor, what are you seeing brother?

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:40.825
Wait, let me switch the glasses.

00:11:40.825 --> 00:11:43.786
Oh, we're getting, like,
a nice croissant shape.

00:11:43.786 --> 00:11:47.039
You know, I think it's called a crescent.

00:11:47.039 --> 00:11:48.207
No no no no no no no.

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:51.085
A croissant, it's like very thick.

00:11:51.085 --> 00:11:51.961
You check it out. Check it.

00:11:54.714 --> 00:11:55.089
Yeah. No.

00:11:55.089 --> 00:11:55.631
It's cool.

00:11:55.631 --> 00:11:57.425
Here we take those same cardboard
cutouts

00:11:57.425 --> 00:11:58.426
Here's the one with the triangle.

00:11:58.426 --> 00:12:00.261
And again, you're seeing a triangle.

00:12:00.261 --> 00:12:03.222
But look what happens when I move it away.

00:12:03.222 --> 00:12:04.598
Look, it's a crescent.

00:12:04.598 --> 00:12:07.268
How cool is that? Okay, and
I can do it with another shape.

00:12:07.268 --> 00:12:11.439
I'm gonna try with this star shape,
which again, close up.

00:12:11.439 --> 00:12:14.942
But if I move away? This star...

00:12:14.942 --> 00:12:16.152
Also a crescent.

00:12:16.152 --> 00:12:17.695
How sick is that?

00:12:17.695 --> 00:12:18.654
You can even do it.

00:12:18.654 --> 00:12:20.906
With something like this,
with all these little circular holes.

00:12:20.906 --> 00:12:24.118
Circle. That's a circle. But even quicker.

00:12:24.118 --> 00:12:24.869
A bunch of 

00:12:24.869 --> 00:12:26.287
little crescents.

00:12:26.287 --> 00:12:28.831
That's amazing. Right? Come on, come on.

00:12:28.831 --> 00:12:31.709
See, when the cardboard is high enough,
you're no longer looking at the hole,

00:12:31.709 --> 00:12:34.503
but a projection of the light source
itself.

00:12:34.503 --> 00:12:38.132
Light travels in straight lines,
so a ray leaving the top of the sun has to

00:12:38.132 --> 00:12:39.842
angle downward to get through the hole.

00:12:39.842 --> 00:12:42.344
So it lands at the bottom here.

00:12:42.344 --> 00:12:45.264
The top goes to the bottom
and the left flips to the right.

00:12:45.264 --> 00:12:49.435
Everything crosses at the hole and
it comes out on the other side reversed,

00:12:49.435 --> 00:12:51.979
which means that during an eclipse,
the crescents on the ground

00:12:51.979 --> 00:12:54.690
point the opposite
way to the crescents in the sky.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:57.443
All cameras actually work like this,
flipping your image.

00:12:57.443 --> 00:12:59.987
But to get this effect,
you need the point to be rather small,

00:12:59.987 --> 00:13:01.655
it's like a pinhole camera.

00:13:01.655 --> 00:13:04.658
That's why all the spaces between leaves
and a tree are perfect.

00:13:04.658 --> 00:13:07.828
During an eclipse,
they all become crescents.

00:13:07.828 --> 00:13:09.663
And there's another crazy effect.

00:13:09.663 --> 00:13:10.873
Watch my hand.

00:13:10.873 --> 00:13:14.376
The shadows around my fingers
are quite fuzzy, but if I turn my hand

00:13:14.376 --> 00:13:18.631
90 degrees, those same shadows
suddenly become much sharper.

00:13:18.631 --> 00:13:20.174
As we approach totality,

00:13:20.174 --> 00:13:24.512
every object has a sharp direction
and a soft one, 90 degrees apart.

00:13:24.512 --> 00:13:28.224
Take one point source of light, on its own
it casts a perfectly sharp shadow,

00:13:28.224 --> 00:13:32.353
but add a second point, also perfectly
sharp, but arriving at a different angle.

00:13:32.353 --> 00:13:35.064
Now, where the two shadows overlap,
you get full darkness,

00:13:35.064 --> 00:13:37.233
but out at the edges,
where only one of them lands,

00:13:37.233 --> 00:13:40.110
you get half the light.
If you do it for more and more points,

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.363
well, those edges
start to stack up into a gradient,

00:13:42.363 --> 00:13:44.782
and that's what makes the blur.

00:13:44.782 --> 00:13:48.661
And during an eclipse, you're left with
this sliver shape. In the tall direction,

00:13:48.661 --> 00:13:50.704
it stays roughly the same width as normal.

00:13:50.704 --> 00:13:53.624
That's why in this direction
you still get blurry shadows.

00:13:53.624 --> 00:13:58.087
But change your orientation 90 degrees
and now, it's much more narrow.

00:13:58.087 --> 00:14:00.005
So now it's like a point source again.

00:14:00.005 --> 00:14:03.259
That's why the shadows in this direction
are perfectly sharp.

00:14:07.096 --> 00:14:09.098
So we're a few minutes from totality.

00:14:09.098 --> 00:14:11.517
And, what we're going to do
is we're going to hold up this sheet,

00:14:11.517 --> 00:14:14.728
and what we're looking for
are these long moving shadows.

00:14:14.728 --> 00:14:17.523
They're sometimes called snake shadows
because of how they move.

00:14:17.523 --> 00:14:19.024
So you're talking about seconds

00:14:19.024 --> 00:14:22.236
before totality
that you'll see these shadow bands occur

00:14:22.236 --> 00:14:25.197
so maybe 20s, 10s before totality.

00:14:25.197 --> 00:14:27.408
And so you really have to be, you know,

00:14:27.408 --> 00:14:29.743
looking for them and aware
that they're going to exist.

